7.2 The circuit

qftCircuit = (n) =&gt; {
  let c = new ket.Circuit(n)
  ket.qft(c, Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) =&gt; i))
  return c
}
svg(qftCircuit(4).toSVG())















The structure: H on each qubit, followed by controlled phase rotations from subsequent qubits (angles \(\pi/2^k\) decreasing), then bit-order reversal swaps. For \(n=4\): 10 gates. Classical FFT on 16 points: 32 multiplications.

7.3 Phase estimation
Phase estimation is the QFT’s most important application. Given a unitary \(U\) with eigenstate \(|\psi\rangle\) satisfying \(U|\psi\rangle = e^{2\pi i\phi}|\psi\rangle\), it recovers \(\phi\) to \(n\) bits of precision using \(n\) ancilla qubits and \(O(2^n)\) applications of \(U\).
The S gate has eigenvalue \(e^{i\pi/2} = e^{2\pi i \cdot 1/4}\) on \(|1\rangle\) — phase \(\phi = 1/4\). With 4 precision qubits, the encoded integer is \(\phi \cdot 2^4 = 4 = 0100_2\). In ket’s qubit ordering (qubit 0 is LSB), this registers as ancilla bits 0010. The histogram shows all 5 qubits — the last bit is the target staying in \(|1\rangle\):

qpe_s = {
  let c = new ket.Circuit(5).x(4)             // target qubit in eigenstate |1⟩
  for (let k = 0; k &lt; 4; k++) c = c.h(k)     // superpose ancillas
  for (let k = 0; k &lt; 4; k++)                 // controlled-S^(2^k)
    for (let p = 0; p &lt; 2 ** k; p++) c = c.cs(k, 4)
  return ket.applyIqft(c, 4, 0)               // inverse QFT
}
svg(qpe_s.toSVG())
















svg(qpe_s.simulate({ shots: 500 }).toSVG())






00101 with certainty — the 4 ancilla bits read 0010, meaning qubit 2 is set. In LSB-first encoding that’s integer 4, and \(4/16 = 1/4\). Phase recovered exactly. One run, four bits of precision.

8.6 Try it — QPE factoring
This demo runs a genuine full-statevector QPE simulation: every amplitude in the \(2^{t+n}\)-dimensional Hilbert space is allocated and updated on each gate. For large \(N\) the state lives in GPU VRAM rather than the JS heap, reaching state spaces of up to \(2^{31}\) amplitudes (~16 GB) on high-end hardware.
What is specifically chosen and why. The targets are twin prime semiprimes: \(N = p(p+2)\) where \(p\) and \(p+2\) are both prime, and the base \(a = p+1\). This guarantees \(a^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{N}\), so the period is always \(r = 2\). With \(r = 2\) a single counting bit (\(t = 1\)) suffices, halving the state space compared to general targets. These are not arbitrary RSA numbers — they are chosen so the simulation stays tractable.
What the simulation is genuinely doing. Despite the favorable inputs, the QPE circuit itself is unmodified and uncompiled: H gates are applied over all \(2^{t+n}\) amplitudes, the controlled-multiplication runs as a real quantum gate, and the inverse QFT extracts the phase. Every quantum amplitude is computed honestly. Early quantum hardware demonstrations that “factored 15” typically used circuits hand-compiled to near-trivial form for a specific \(N\) and \(a\). This demo runs the same algorithm on numbers up to 30 bits — the same circuit a real fault-tolerant quantum computer would execute.
The real ceiling. Factoring cryptographically relevant numbers (\(n \geq 2048\)) requires a fault-tolerant quantum computer with thousands of logical qubits. No such machine exists. The GPU simulation here is as far as exact classical simulation of this circuit can go.

// n = work qubits needed (2^n &gt; N) = bit-length of N.
// Each step doubles the state space: 2^(t+n) amplitudes, ~1 GB per 3 extra qubits.
SHOR_TARGETS = [
  { N: 15,       a: 2,      aSq: 4,   n: 4,  label: "15 = 3 × 5" },
  { N: 77,       a: 34,     aSq: 1,   n: 7,  label: "77 = 7 × 11" },
  { N: 989,      a: 300,    aSq: 1,   n: 10, label: "989 = 23 × 43" },
  { N: 2047,     a: 390,    aSq: 622, n: 11, label: "2047 = 23 × 89" },
  { N: 2701,     a: 2554,   aSq: 1,   n: 12, label: "2701 = 37 × 73" },
  { N: 10403,    a: 102,    aSq: 1,   n: 14, label: "10403 = 101 × 103" },
  { N: 34571,    a: 27694,  aSq: 1,   n: 16, label: "34571 = 181 × 191" },
  { N: 72899,    a: 270,    aSq: 1,   n: 17, label: "72899 = 269 × 271" },
  { N: 1005973,  a: 838478, aSq: 1,   n: 20, label: "1005973 = 997 × 1009" },
  { N: 4112783,  a: 2028,   aSq: 1,   n: 22, label: "4112783 = 2027 × 2029" },
  { N: 11902499, a: 3450,   aSq: 1,   n: 24, label: "11902499 = 3449 × 3451" },
  { N: 23912099,  a: 4890,  aSq: 1, n: 25, t: 1, label: "23912099 = 4889 × 4891  (~10s)" },
  { N: 47609999,  a: 6900,  aSq: 1, n: 26, t: 1, label: "47609999 = 6899 × 6901  (~30s)" },
  { N: 98604899,  a: 9930,  aSq: 1, n: 27, t: 1, label: "98604899 = 9929 × 9931  (~2 min)" },
  { N: 267715043, a: 16362, aSq: 1, n: 28, t: 1, label: "267715043 = 16361 × 16363  (~8 min)" },
  { N: 531671363, a: 23058, aSq: 1, n: 29, t: 1, label: "531671363 = 23057 × 23059  (~16 min)" },
  { N: 1070467523,  a: 32718, aSq: 1, n: 30, t: 1, label: "1070467523 = 32717 × 32719  (16 GB VRAM, RTX 4090+)" },
  { N: 2144430863,  a: 46308, aSq: 1, n: 31, t: 1, label: "2144430863 = 46307 × 46309  (32 GB VRAM)" },
